    "case_id": "313",
    "case_summary": "A healthy male toddler presented to the emergency department with abdominal pain and distention, fever, and vomiting of 1 day's duration. He was found to have 'bruises' on both flanks. Over the next 2 days, he developed diffuse papules on the trunk and extremities; his condition rapidly deteriorated requiring intubation, vasopressors, and empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics.\n\nPurpuric retiform patches were found on each flank (1 of them studded with 2 pustules) with erythematous to violaceous papules diffusely scattered on the extremities and trunk.\n\n- Laboratory tests:  - Hyponatremia and neutropenia present  - AST: 45 U/L  - ALT: 142 U/L   - C-reactive protein: >270 mg/L  - Erythrocyte sedimentation rate: 43 mm/h  - Ferritin: 603 ng/mL  - Urinalysis showed proteinuria and hematuria- Imaging studies:  - CT chest/abdomen: Cavitary lesions in lungs representing necrotizing pneumonia. Areas of nonenhancement within both kidneys suspected to represent renal abscesses  - MRI brain: Multifocal lesions, likely septic emboli  - Transthoracic echocardiogram: Negative  - Bone scans: Negative- Pathological tests:  - Punch biopsy findings:    - Frozen section: Small organisms within vasculature    - Permanent sections: Large neutrophilic pustule with inflammation throughout dermis and prominent necrosis with basophilic structures filling necrotic vascular spaces    - Tissue Gram staining: Gram-positive cocci in vessels  - Tissue culture, blood culture, bronchial washings, and fluid from bilateral empyemas all showed positive results",
